A Go module whose only vulnerability is in the Go standard library (net/http),
fixable only by bumping the go directive. The intended
CVE is GO-2024-2687 / CVE-2023-45288 (HTTP/2 CONTINUATION flood), fixed in
go1.21.9; the desired remediation is go.mod `go 1.21.0` -> `go 1.21.9`.

EXPECTED: FAIL.

GAP: the vulncheck bot script cannot remediate standard-library vulnerabilities.
It only knows how to `go get` module versions, but stdlib fixes come from the Go
toolchain/go directive. With GOTOOLCHAIN=local and an installed go1.26, the
scan is evaluated against the running toolchain, so it never even reports the
go1.21.9 fix; instead it reports newer stdlib CVEs whose `Fixed in:` lines look
like `crypto/tls@go1.26.5`. The awk matches those (they contain `@`) and the
script runs `go get crypto/tls@go1.26.5`, which errors ("can't request explicit
version of standard library package"), short-circuits the `&& go mod tidy`, and
leaves the tree unchanged. The script therefore produces an EMPTY diff while the
want_diff bumps the go directive, so the case fails.
-- go.mod --
module example.com/foo

go 1.21.0
-- main.go --
package main

import "net/http"

// net/http is the vulnerable stdlib package (GO-2024-2687); the only fix is a
// go-directive bump, which the bot cannot perform.
func main() {
	http.Get("http://example.com/")
}
-- want_diff.txt --
diff --git a/go.mod b/go.mod
index 9b981c3..d7e9510 100644
--- a/go.mod
+++ b/go.mod
@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@
 module example.com/foo
 
-go 1.21.0
+go 1.21.9
